SIGSEGVs under BIT_GC=stress against a Bit-sourced libbitrt.a were tracked as
#1835 and are FIXED by #3246: `ckeys` (tests/stress/rootbit/rootbit.bit) held
`mine()`'s return values — real port-heap string addresses — in an untraced
`[]i64`, so the collector reused blocks the program still meant to read 1396
events later. The fix rebuilds `ckeys` as a port ref-slice
(`rtSliceNew(..., isRef: 1, 8)`, the same mechanism `runtime/root/maps.bit` uses
for its own keys buffer) so the collector traces every slot. Verified 5/5
`BIT_GC=stress` rc=0, mutation-proven: reverting only the holder back to
`[]i64` reproduces the identical SIGSEGV 5/5, restoring the fix clears it 5/5
again.

#3246 IS FIXED AND THIS FILE MUST STAY — a second, independent failure survives
the fix and is tracked as #3245: `phaseI` (rootbit.bit:381-399) stores a raw
port-heap string byte pointer into `meta`, another `[]i64` that is untraced for
the identical reason, and the crash in `ckeys` was masking it. With the crash
gone, `BIT_GC=stress` now prints `ifc=16/17` on 2 of 5 runs (verified while
closing #3246) instead of the `17/17` `BIT_GC=off` and `rootbit.expected` both
show. Every other field — `wy`, `str`, `slc`, `map`, `null` — is correct 5/5,
which is itself evidence `ckeys` is clean: `map`'s strong assertion
(`cAgree`, phaseM) walks the exact tombstone-flood sequence `ckeys` drives,
slot by slot against a native map, and it agreed on all 5 runs.

Why this program and no other: rootbit tests the object model BELOW the type
system, so every intermediate it holds is a raw `int` address rather than a
typed reference, and #3245's `meta` still lives in module-level `[]i64`
storage that is untraced by definition. Nothing in the root set enumerates
"addresses a program stashed somewhere the tracer was told not to look", and
nothing should.

What the waiver costs, stated plainly rather than implied: `phaseI`'s 17
checks, intermittently, under `BIT_GC=stress` only. Until G3 this program
allocated from a private heap the live runtime collector had never been told
about, so not one of its objects was ever a collection candidate and every
assertion it has ever made was made with the collector effectively off.
Sharing the live heap is what first subjected it to real collection: `ckeys`
did not survive that and is now fixed (#3246); `meta` still does not (open as
#3245). `BIT_GC=off` is therefore not a downgrade from coverage that existed —
it is the coverage this program has always had beyond `ckeys`, now labelled.

Do not remove this marker by giving rootbit its private heap back. That
restores the inertness above and hides #3245's answer: either a real root-set
hole in the shipped runtime, or `phaseI` cannot hold descriptor name bytes
across a collection either and must say so, the same way #1835 already
answered that question for `ckeys`.
